---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase, CONTACT_LINK, CONTACT_LABEL } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Services"
  description="Three ways to work with KimuDev: a fast templated Starter build, a fully custom build (sites, apps, mini apps, PWAs, scripts), or maintenance and fixes on something that already exists."
>
  <section class="section page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Three ways to work with us</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Most projects need one of three things: something working
        fast, something built exactly around how you operate, or
        someone to keep an existing site or app actually running.
        We don't force any of those into the others' box.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap service-block">
      <div class="service-heading">
        <h2>Starter</h2>
        <p class="service-for">For a first website, a booking/ordering flow, or an MVP to test an idea.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="service-body">
        <p>
          We start from a template we already trust — proven layout,
          proven performance — and fit it to your business: your
          content, your branding, your copy. Nothing about the result
          looks or reads like a template once it's done.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A short intake conversation to scope pages and content</li>
          <li>Your own branding, copy, and images throughout</li>
          <li>Basic contact/booking flow if your business needs one</li>
          <li>Live in a matter of weeks, not months</li>
          <li>One round of revisions included before launch</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap service-block">
      <div class="service-heading">
        <h2>Custom build</h2>
        <p class="service-for">Sites, apps, mini apps, PWAs, or scripts built for one specific job.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="service-body">
        <p>
          This is a real build, not a configuration exercise. We scope
          the actual problem first — what it runs on, who uses it,
          what has to be true for launch — before any code gets
          written. It's the track behind
          <a href={withBase('https://teredatrades.github.io/Bejirond/')}>Bejirond</a>,
          our offline-first, seven-language expense manager, and the
          rest of the work in our <a href={withBase('work/')}>portfolio</a>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A scoping call before any commitment — free, no pressure</li>
          <li>A written plan: what's being built, in what order, and why</li>
          <li>Covers full apps and sites as well as smaller scope: a mini app, a PWA, or a script built for one specific environment or task</li>
          <li>Architecture decisions made upfront, not discovered mid-build</li>
          <li>Support after launch, not a handoff-and-disappear</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap service-block">
      <div class="service-heading">
        <h2>Maintenance &amp; fixes</h2>
        <p class="service-for">For a site or app that already exists — yours or someone else's build.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="service-body">
        <p>
          Not everything needs a rebuild. Sometimes a site just needs
          something fixed, an app needs a feature added without
          breaking what already works, or an old codebase needs
          reorganizing before anyone can safely touch it again. We
          take that on too — on projects we built, and on ones we
          didn't.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Bug fixes and small feature additions to a live site or app</li>
          <li>Reorganizing or cleaning up an existing codebase</li>
          <li>Ongoing maintenance so a project doesn't quietly rot after launch</li>
          <li>Honest scoping if the real answer turns out to be a rebuild</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cta-section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Not sure which one fits?</h2>
      <p class="lede">That's a normal question to have before you've scoped anything — tell us what you're building and we'll tell you honestly.</p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CONTACT_LINK} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{CONTACT_LABEL}</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .page-hero { padding-bottom: 1rem; }
    .service-block {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 0.85fr 1.15fr;
      gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
      align-items: start;
    }
    .service-for { color: var(--text-muted); max-width: 32ch; }
    .service-body ul {
      margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
      padding-left: 1.1em;
      color: var(--text-muted);
    }
    .service-body li { margin-bottom: 0.6em; }
    .service-body p { max-width: 56ch; }
    @media (max-width: 780px) {
      .service-block { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }
    .cta-section { padding-bottom: clamp(4.5rem, 10vw, 8rem); }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
